\begin{lemma}[A separated immersed annular minimum]
\label{lem:annular-immersed-minimum}
\uses{lem:annular-weak-minimum,lem:annular-uniformization}
\lean{PoincareMT.M64.auxiliaryCircle_free_ramp_immersed_minimum}
\lean{PoincareMT.M64Uniformization.exists_lipschitz_free_modulus_conformal_cylinder}
The separated ramps of Lemma~\ref{lem:annular-weak-minimum}
admit an immersed minimal annulus realizing their admissible area
infimum, with the actual boundary curves up to boundary labels.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Smooth positive approximations to the Gram matrix of any Lipschitz
competitor, followed by annular uniformization, show that the weak
minimum's energy is at most that competitor's area plus an
arbitrarily small error. Stationarity in both the modulus and the
boundary labels kills the conformal stress. Interior and boundary
regularity give the same harmonic conformal map; the positive
phase derivative excludes branches. Hence it is immersed and its
area equals its energy and the guarded area infimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:annular-evolution}]
\proves{prop:annular-evolution}
Apply the separated minimum at each time. The Gauss equation and
boundary shortening variation give the upper forward comparison
with coefficient $(2n-1)A_{\sup}(t)$. The finite-time comparison
uses near-minimizers at the initial time, so it does not assert
differentiability of a family of minimizers.

Projection shows $\mu(t)\leq\mu_\varepsilon(t)$. Lifting any original
Lipschitz competitor by the height $\varepsilon y$ increases its
area by at most $C_U\varepsilon$. Thus
$\mu_\varepsilon(t)\to\mu(t)$. Swept boundary annuli and metric
comparison give nonemptiness and continuity. Integrate the
separated forward inequality first and only then pass to this
pointwise limit. On shrinking right intervals, continuity of
$A_{\sup}$ gives \eqref{eq:annulus-forward}. Its constant-coefficient
version gives \eqref{eq:annulus-exponential}. A derivative inequality
alone cannot be passed directly through a pointwise limit.
\end{proof}
\source{MT2007}\dcref{Lemma 19.15}
\dcref{Corollary 19.16, pp. 447--449}

